package cosmonic:memory-demo@0.1.0;

/// The private, in-host interface to the `parser` component.
///
/// `parser` is a C record decoder with a deliberate out-of-bounds write
/// (CWE-787). It takes the untrusted record as plain bytes and hands back a
/// JSON report. Note what the `parser` world below does NOT have: an import.
/// There is no function in its world that names another component's memory,
/// a process, a file or a socket. That absence is the boundary this demo
/// tests: the write can run as far as the parser's own linear memory goes,
/// and no further.
interface parsing {
  /// Decode one record and report what the decoder did.
  ///
  /// `ok`  is a JSON report (decoded length, how far past its buffer the
  ///       decoder wrote, and the state of the parser's own adjacent buffer).
  /// `err` is a plain message for a record the decoder refused (bad magic,
  ///       truncated header). An out-of-bounds write that leaves the linear
  ///       memory returns neither: the instance traps.
  parse: func(%record: list<u8>) -> result<string, string>;
}

/// The private, in-host interface to the `vault` component: a sibling in the
/// same workload that holds a secret in ITS OWN linear memory.
interface secrets {
  /// Return the secret the vault holds. The demo calls this before and after
  /// the parser's overflow to show the bytes never changed.
  reveal: func() -> string;
}

/// The vulnerable parser: exports `parsing` and imports nothing.
world parser {
  export parsing;
}

/// The vault: exports `secrets` and imports nothing.
world vault {
  export secrets;
}

/// The web component: imports both interfaces so it can drive the parser and
/// read the vault. Its HTTP entrypoint (wasi:http/handler) is generated
/// separately by the `wasip3` crate, so it is not declared here.
world web {
  import parsing;
  import secrets;
}
